3. Checks on Literature Review 1
3.1 Statements confirmed against the pages read
Zhang: thermal saturation time 20 s; line of sight drawn as Bernoulli with p = 0.8;
nominal atmospheric transmittance about 0.57 (visibility 20 km, range 8 km, elevation
10 degrees); threat type (recon, decoy, strike) given in the UAV state; domain
randomization named as future work; CPLEX mentioned only in the related-work
section. [DOC]
Li: two weapons against four UAVs; energy budget 15, firing cost 3, recharge 0.5; laser
damage linear in range and time (Eq. 13) in normalized units; reactive dodge with
probability 0.15; 8 seeds with 1000 deterministic evaluation episodes each, Wilcoxon
and Mann-Whitney tests; the abstract states that rule-based scheduling with exact
geometric pointing yields more raw interceptions than the learned methods. [DOC]
Li also treats UAV behavior type and maneuver phase as directly observable, calls this
an idealized upper bound, and leaves a belief over behavior type to future work. This
supports gap G2. [DOC, Li Section 2.2]
3.2 Corrections and additions
Edwards 2021 (now read in full). It is a supervised classification study, not a
scheduling policy. It uses the NPS Swarm Commander Tactics simulator with one ship,
a 10 kW laser with 300 energy units, engagements under 5 km, and laser performance
from the NPS ANCHOR scaling code. A decision tree predicts win or loss of the ship
under two fixed strategies, proximity-first and threat-first. Baseline battles (273 runs,
Table 4): the ship won 106 of 161 under proximity-first (65.8%) and 100 of 112 under
threat-first (89.3%). Final model (Table 7): 229 of 244 proximity cases and 175 of 176
threat cases correct, which gives 404 of 420 = 96.2%, matching the reported 96%.
[DOC; percentages recomputed from the table counts]
Taylor 2021. Now read in full (95 pages; details in Section 4.2). Master's thesis,
December 2021, Systems Engineering, NPS; advisor B. W. Johnson, co-advisor J. M.
Green. Title: Counter-Unmanned Aerial Vehicles Study: Shipboard Laser Weapon
