41.30% (RL), a reduction of 17.96% relative to the heuristic. Review 1 uses version 2
values (52.14% and 40.70%). The Review 1 phrase 'heuristic is 22% worse than RL'
should read 'RL reduces average damage by about 22% relative to the heuristic' (11.44
/ 52.14 = 21.9%). Name the version when citing. [REF for v1 values; DOC for the v2
values in Review 1]
Hanak 2025 (cross-entropy laser defense), DOI 10.2514/1.I011357 [REF]. Its
introduction says that, to the authors' knowledge, WTA for laser-based defense had
not been addressed. Zhang (Section 2.2) cites laser-specific assignment work [18-21]
and the NPS laser studies of 2015-2021 in Section 4 predate it, so treat novelty claims
in this niche with caution.
Wei 2026. Authors Wei, Wang, Wang, Hong (Beijing Institute of Technology), DOI
10.15918/j.tbit1001-0645.2026.003. The abstract names PPO with self-attention and
hierarchical masking and claims good generalization and robustness, with no
numbers. The figures in Review 1 (92.1%, 90.0%, 50.0%) were not re-checked. [REF]
4. New literature
4.1 Chen, Xu, Szechtman, Yan and Vanterpool, arXiv:2607.13648v2 (16 July
2026)[DOC]
The closest theoretical neighbor found. The model: n threats; C effector shots per round;
each shot neutralizes threat i independently with probability 1 - q_i; the defender sees
which threats survive only at the end of each round. Three objectives: expected time to
clear all threats, probability of clearing all threats by a deadline, and effective (nonwasted) assignments by a deadline.
Main results as stated by the authors: spreading shots evenly across live threats (fair
allocation) is optimal when threats are homogeneous or C is at most 2 (Theorem 2); when
C grows at least in proportion to n, its expected clearance time exceeds the optimum by
at most an additive constant independent of n (Theorem 5); difficulty-aware greedy
policies are computed by steepest ascent, with a 1 - 1/e guarantee for the effectiveassignment objective (Theorem 9) and no constant-factor guarantee for the survivalprobability greedy policy (Remark 2). Bellman optima were computed only for small cases
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 4 of 25

(n up to 7, deadline of 2 rounds). The conclusion reports greedy policies within 1% of the
optimum under their aligned objectives; the larger cases (n up to 30) compare heuristics
with each other.
Differences from our setting: kills are memoryless shots, so there is no dwell
accumulation; difficulties q_i are known to the greedy policies; there are no slewing,
energy or thermal limits; no learned policies are compared.
Implications [OPEN, my reading]:
The rule set must include fair allocation and difficulty-aware greedy (marginal-return)
rules, not only nearest-target and time-to-breach rules.
Resource tightness C/n is a ready-made regime variable. A dwell-based analogue
(required dwell time against available time before breach) is a candidate axis for the
regime map.
If simple policies stay near-optimal over most of the stylized space, H2 is harder to
confirm. Review 1 already flags H2 as the main risk.
The operations research community is active on this problem (first version July 2026).
The related-work section must cover it, and the choice between OR journals and
aerospace orAI journals needs a decision.
4.2 Taylor 2021, NPS master's thesis (read in full)[DOC]
A simulation study in the NPS Swarm Commander Tactics (SCT) simulator of a shipboard
laser against bomber swarms. The laser: 100 kW, 0.3 m beam director, yaw 90 degrees/s
and pitch 45 degrees/s, 300 energy units used at 1 unit/s, propagation from the ANCHOR
scaling code. One ship defends; the SCT build used cannot change the atmosphere, aim
points or target materials.
Two experiments. First, formation and size: dot (an impossible best case), line, wedge and
waved wedge, for 50, 100 and 200 bombers, one row per scenario (Table 11). Excluding the
dot case, the ship was destroyed in 0 of 3, 1 of 3 and 3 of 3 scenarios at 50, 100 and 200
bombers (Table 12: 0%, 33%, 100%). The author attributes the line formation's success to
longer slew times between angularly separated targets. Second, armored decoys (fighters
with three times the health, modeled as three co-located units) replace a quarter of a
waved-wedge swarm, under proximity-first and take-out-the-shooter targeting. At 200
units the ship was destroyed in all three settings, but the share of bombers defeated was
62.5% (no decoys, proximity), 2.7% (decoys, proximity) and 85.3% (decoys, shooter only).
Recomputed from Table 13: 75 of 200 bombers left, 146 of 150 left, 22 of 150 left.
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 5 of 25

Five engagement strategies are proposed: proximity; shortest engagement (expected
slew time plus expected dwell time, choose the minimum); take out the shooter; focus on
information nodes; maximize soft kill. Only proximity and take-out-the-shooter were
simulated. The shooter strategy needs correct identification of the payload carriers, and
the thesis notes that radar cross-section or imagery cues can become unreliable if the
adversary modifies the platforms. The simulator assumes perfect sensing, perfect battle
damage assessment and no tracking time. The tables show one row per scenario and I
found no mention of replication or variance (the absence is my reading).
Implications [OPEN, my reading]:
The armored decoy is a hardness-heterogeneity case in which the better strategy
wins only if the class is known. It is a concrete, citable motivation for G2.
Add the shortest-engagement rule (slew plus expected dwell) to the rule set. It is the
closest published rule to a time-based index.
Use the thesis as qualitative support only: single runs and perfect information.
Parameter pointer: Taylor cites Michnewich (2018, NPS thesis) for about 44.4 kJ to
destroy a UAV with a 2 mm aluminum skin and about 4 MJ of stored energy per
engagement at 6 km [REF, second-hand]. Useful for sanity checks on energy
parameters.
Leads, unread: Carr 2021 (NPS thesis on integrated laser and kinetic weapon selection
and scheduling, listed as forthcoming by Taylor) and Blickley et al. 2021 (NPS capstone
on a cognitive laser decision aid). Two articles by B. Johnson describe them as
covering laser-plus-kinetic engagement logic and machine learning for the required
dwell time from target material [REF]. Both touch G1 and G2.
4.3 Sung et al., IEEE Access (read in full from the project file)[DOC]
Cluster-Based Weapon-Target Assignment for Large-Scale Drone Swarms; Sung, Lee,
Song, Kim, Kwon, Cho; received 29 May 2026; DOI 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3712831; pages
105236-105256. It solves a static assignment of combat drones (single-shot, dual-shot,
one-way attack) to targets as a mixed-integer nonlinear program (WTA-D), and
decomposes large instances by clustering targets (k-means, a value-balanced integer
program, or a p-median) and matching drone groups to clusters.
Relevance: none to directed energy. The drones are the weapons, kill probabilities are
assumed known with complete reliability, battery use is linear in distance, and there is no
dwell, slewing or thermal model. This settles the open question in the first version of this
document: it is not a competitor for G1. Three points are useful:
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 6 of 25

Scale limits of exact static solution: the exact formulation took a mean of 292.05 s on
30-by-30 instances against a 300 s limit, and at 99-by-99 only 1 of 30 instances
reached a solution without slack activation within 3600 s. The clustered variants run
in about 6 s (PVBC: mean penalized objective 24.57 in 5.78 s), which the authors
describe as a trade against solution quality. Relevant to receding-horizon baselines at
N = 100 to 300 [OPEN]. The limit applies to Sung's MINLP with range and dual-shot
constraints; Bertsimas and Paskov (Section 4.4) report exact solution of classical
static WTA at 10,000 weapons and targets on a laptop. Their hardware was an Intel i5-
8250U laptop with 8 GB RAM, BARON with 6 threads, GAMS 45.2 and MATLAB
R2022b, comparable to our compute.
Reporting practice: 30 paired instances per group, a Friedman test, then Holmcorrected two-sided Wilcoxon tests. Weaknesses to avoid: GA and PSO were run once
per instance, and the objective mixes residual value with slack penalties.
Reference-list leads [REF]: Nan et al. (2022), Electronics 11(11):1796, RL-IGWO for
dynamic WTA in frigate defense against UAV swarms; Xin et al. (2011), IEEE Trans.
SMC-A 41(3):598-606, rule-based constructive heuristic for dynamic WTA; Lu and
Chen (2021), Omega 98:102138, and Andersen, Pavlikov, Toffolo (2022), Ann. Oper. Res.
312(2):581-606, exact WTA algorithms; Kim, Lee, Yi (2022), IEEE Access 10:43738-
43750, clustering-based WTA with heading error.
4.5 Glazebrook, Kirkbride, Mitchell, Gaver and Jacobs: Index policies for
shooting problems (NPS technical report NPS-OR-06-004, January 2006; read in
full)[DOC]
A single shooter (Red) faces N targets (Blue) in a discounted Markov decision problem. At
each step Red shoots one target or disengages for a fixed return Rd. Theorem 1: a Gittinstype index policy is optimal, and Red disengages when every target's index is below Rd.
Each index is the maximum, over stopping times, of expected reward divided by a risk
term (Eq. 8).
Three models are analysed. In Model 1, targets have B types, each with its own kill
probability, retaliation probability and withdrawal probability; Red holds a prior over each
target's type and updates it by Bayes' rule after every inconclusive shot (Eq. 11), so
repeated failure shifts belief toward harder types. In Model 2, targets accumulate damage
over D observable states under a known Markov law, with a closed-form index. In Model 3,
an imperfect inspection follows each shot (shoot-look-shoot). Section 4 values
disengagement under raids and Poisson arrivals: the more future gain available, the more
selective the optimal shooter.
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 7 of 25

Numerical study: 10^6 runs per policy and problem set, with N = 10 targets, B = 5 types
and a survival discount of 0.95. The index policy has the highest mean return, as the
theory requires. It nearly coincides with the exhaustive policy (shoot each target until one
side is dead) when priors are informative: problem set 2 means are 1126.04 and 1125.96
(Table 2). Optimal disengagement raised the simulated mean return in problem set 1 from
307.51 to 340.65 at m = 0.95 (Table 3).
Differences from our setting: one shooter with unlimited shots; discounted reward until
the shooter's own demise; targets stay put while not engaged (withdrawal while
unengaged makes the problem a restless bandit, which the authors call likely intractable
and handle with Whittle-type heuristics); no breach deadlines; switching penalties appear
only through other work, where strict optimality is lost; no learned policies.
Implications [OPEN, my reading]:
Learning a hardness class from failed attempts and then persisting or disengaging is
classical here. A novelty claim for our problem must rest on what breaks the bandit
structure in directed-energy engagements: deadlines and approaching targets, slew
or switching time, continuous dwell progress with an unknown threshold, several
effectors, and thermal or energy limits.
The belief index of Model 1 is the right information-matched baseline for any learned
policy.
The finding that the index and a simple exhaustive rule nearly coincide under
informative priors is a ready hypothesis for H2: learning should matter only when
priors are weak or the structure breaks the index.
The journal version (Oper. Res. 55(4):769-781, 2007) was not read; check for
differences before citing equations.
4.6 Other checks in this round
Further directed-energy RL schedulers: two more searches (laser and microwave
scheduling with reinforcement learning, 2026) returned only Zhang, Li and items
already listed. Not exhaustive [OPEN]. A snippet of Li reports that the laser and
microwave target the same UAV in 34% of firing frames [REF].
Safety physics [REF]: Azarian et al. (Fraunhofer IOSB) measured reflections of a highenergy laser from melting steel targets and report a worst-case nominal ocular hazard
distance of 560 m for a 100 kW laser (Fraunhofer record of a 2026 conference paper;
an Optica abstract gives the same figure, journal and authors not captured). Daigle et
al., Optical Engineering 56:026106 (2017), argue for hazard zones based on measured
