divergence instead of the flat-mirror worst case. Schwenger, Azarian, Henrichsen,
Applied Optics 63(14):3825-3841 (2024), simulate beam reflection at the sea surface.
None is a scheduling paper. Kumru 2023 (Review 1) was not located.
Khalil et al. (RMIT), arXiv:2510.16495: the introduction snippet says laser and kinetic
systems are inherently sequential and that high-power microwave systems have an
effectively unlimited magazine subject to power and cooling [REF].
Heuristics for the stochastic dynamic task-resource allocation problem with retry
opportunities, European Journal of Operational Research (CORE record; authors and
year not captured): two-stage allocation where first-stage outcomes are observed
before the second stage [REF].
Naval Engineers Journal, Winter 2025, NPS paper on coordinating kinetic and
directed-energy effects: an ASNE blog describes engagements as time-driven, with
dwell time, power and cooling, and deconfliction as constraints [REF; paper not read].
Fiedler et al. (2015), NPS capstone (Calhoun hdl 10945/45247): a kill-assessment
concept with near real-time damage assessment and dwell-time determination that
gives a decision point to re-engage the same threat or move to the next target [REF:
abstract snippet]. Relevant to feedback after dwell.
Carr 2021 and Blickley et al. 2021 (NPS): not located; only popular descriptions by B.
Johnson were found.
4.7 Deadline-scheduling leads and parameter sources (snippets only)[REF]
Restless-bandit deadline scheduling: Large-scale Charging of Electric Vehicles: A
Multi-Armed Bandit Approach (arXiv:1605.07578; authors not captured). The snippet
says earliest-deadline-first and least-laxity-first are optimal when completing all tasks
is feasible, and that its Whittle index policy is improved by a less-laxity-and-longerprocessing-time (LLLP) principle. A second paper on an asymptotically optimal
Lagrangian priority policy for stochastic deadline scheduling (NSF PAR 10250769;
authors not captured) reports gains of 1.9-5.9% over a Whittle-type index policy.
Analogy: charging demand with deadlines resembles dwell demand with breach
times, but unknown hardness is absent. Use: earliest-deadline, least-laxity and
Whittle-type baselines.
Michnewich (2018), NPS thesis (DTIC AD1060007): ANCHOR plus a discrete-event
model for laser dwell times and energy storage; the snippet says the dwell data
assume a stationary target and that the maximum engagement time of ten seconds
depends on the cooling system. Parameter pointer; thesis not read.
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
