Sung et al., IEEE Access, cluster-based WTA: read in full; see Section 4.3. Not a
directed-energy paper.
Queueing and stochastic models of directed-energy defense [REF]
Kracman (2025), J. Defense Model. Simul. 22(2):187-195, DOI
10.1177/15485129231161092, first published online 2 May 2023; the publisher page lists it
as restricted access. Full text was not obtainable and a further search found no open
copy, so it is used at abstract level only (publisher and RePEc abstracts). The abstract
describes a queueing and stochastic jump model of networked high-energy-laser
defense that predicts survival probability for several effectors against any number of
threats and compares three collaboration methods; it suggests that superimposing
lasers on one threat may be less effective than one-to-one allocation. The author is at
Australia's Defence Science and Technology Group. Decision: cite for the focused-fire
versus one-to-one question and for queueing-style predictors, flagged as abstractlevel, and do not rely on its details.
Weinberg and Kracman (2021), arXiv:2106.14381. Jump-process survivability model for
armoured vehicle teams under multiple missile threats with directed-energy defense;
Weinberg (2021) MODSIM paper on a queueing approach to collaborative active
protection. Possible sources of analytical predictors for the load axis.
Stochastic scheduling and index policies [REF]
Scully and Terenin, The Gittins Index: A Design Principle for Decision-Making Under
Uncertainty, arXiv:2506.10872 (June 2025). Unknown job sizes with a known size
distribution, preemption allowed. An unknown dwell requirement with a classdependent distribution is the analogue.
Robust Gittins for Stochastic Scheduling (Moseley, Newman, Pruhs, Zhou; April 2025),
arXiv:2504.10743. The abstract states that the standard Gittins policy is not robust:
arbitrarily small perturbations of the true distributions suffice to break it, and the
authors propose a robust modification. The setting is a single machine, preemption
allowed, minimizing expected mean completion time (abstract and introduction
verified on arXiv).
Tabular and Deep Reinforcement Learning for Gittins Index, arXiv:2405.01157.
Caveat: the classical optimality results concern mean completion time or holding cost,
not kills before a deadline. Whether an index rule is near-optimal for our objective is
[OPEN].
Evaluation of learning against heuristics [REF]
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 12 of 25

Wu, Zhao, Arora, Unrealized Expectations: Comparing AI Methods vs Classical
Algorithms for Maximum Independent Set, arXiv:2502.03669v2. Empirical comparison:
leading AI methods are outperformed by the classical KaMIS solver on one CPU, and
some fail to beat a simple degree-based greedy heuristic (abstract verified).
Nath and Kuhnle, arXiv:2406.11897 (MaxCut benchmark): several learned heuristics
fail to beat a naive greedy algorithm, and Tabu Search beats all but one of them
(abstract and introduction verified). Another item listed earlier, arXiv:2310.19990, was
not re-verified and is not relied on.
Caveat: both studies address static graph optimization, not sequential scheduling.
Use them for framing the baseline argument only.
Infrastructure [REF]
Gymnasium, arXiv:2407.17032 (authors from [MEM]: Towers et al.).
Palmas code for the kinetic counter-UAV environment: github.com/alexpalms/deeprlcounter-uav-swarm (URL given in the paper's appendix).
