Compute [OPEN, not measured]: the stylized DP is expected to be light on one laptop at n
up to 5 and a coarse dwell grid. The simulator should be vectorized and its steps per
second measured before any training plan is fixed. RL training would use one randomized
policy per architecture instead of one policy per cell, with training sizes below the largest
evaluation sizes.
Venue note: a strong paper can be written without choosing a venue first, but the
contribution type decides what reviewers expect: OR journals expect optimality or
bounds and exact benchmarks, aerospace journals expect physics validation and
sensitivity, and AI journals expect seeds, ablations and statistics. Candidate A is built to
meet all three, at a higher workload. Scope limits, formatting and page limits can wait.
Journal scopes and rankings are not verified.
10. Final research idea and initial plan (decided 7 November 2026)
10.1 Decisions
Scope: candidate A of Section 9 is approved by the author.
Venue: the supervisor leaves the venue family open. We target the family that fits
best, provided the contribution is real in that field. Venue choice is therefore a design
decision for us (Section 10.8).
Pilot P0 go threshold: 5% regret of the best simple rule against the DP optimum, as
suggested.
Working rules added: code always comes with comments and explanation; git history
instructions are given whenever a repository step is needed; one new chat per phase
or step; project memory kept current.
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 20 of 25

10.2 Working title and central question
Working title [OPEN]: Learning versus index policies for dwell-based directed-energy
scheduling under uncertain target hardness: a regime analysis.
Central question: when does a learned scheduling policy beat strong, informationmatched index and optimization rules for dwell-based directed-energy engagements, and
which measurable conditions predict it?
RQ1. In a small stylized model solved exactly by dynamic programming (DP), how large
is the regret of strong simple rules, and which conditions drive it?
RQ2. Do a few measurable regime variables (load ratio, information about hardness,
slew ratio) predict that regret?
RQ3. In a physics-informed simulator, do the stylized regimes predict where a learned
policy beats the best information-matched rule or optimizer?
RQ4. How do the methods degrade under prior misspecification and shifts in swarm
size and threat mix?
10.3 Contributions and non-claims
C1: a stylized dwell-based model with accumulating damage, an unknown hardness
class, slew time and breach deadlines, solved exactly for small n, with a regret map of
rules.
C2: a regime predictor, validated on C1 and then on the simulator.
C3: a physics-informed simulator (laser and high-power microwave in the core, kinetic
as a stretch goal), information-matched baselines (fair allocation, earliest deadline,
least laxity, shortest engagement, greedy marginal return, belief index, recedinghorizon sampled-scenario optimizer, class-aware oracle), one RL method (setattention PPO with a single central agent), and out-of-distribution tests.
C4: released code, configurations, seeds and checkpoints.
Non-claims: fielded-system accuracy, a new RL architecture, safety-hazard modeling,
kinetic-effector novelty. G1 and G6 enter only as environment features.
10.4 Hypotheses and statistical plan (frozen before the confirmatory runs)
H1: with slack deadlines, small slew and informative priors, the best simple rule has
regret below 5% in the stylized model.
H2: regret grows with load ratio, slew ratio and prior uncertainty, and exceeds 5% in at
least one region. This is the go criterion of P0.
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 21 of 25

H3: in the simulator, the learned policy beats the best information-matched rule
where stylized regret is at least 5% and does not where it is below.
H4: under prior misspecification, the belief index loses more than a domainrandomized learned policy (motivated by Robust Gittins) [OPEN].
Descriptive only: decision latency of each method.
Statistics:
Seed is the unit for RL (10 training seeds). Rules are compared on identical evaluation
episodes, and episodes are paired.
At most 8 primary confirmatory contrasts, with Holm correction. Everything else is
reported as effect sizes with confidence intervals and labelled secondary or
exploratory.
With 10 seeds the smallest one-sided exact Wilcoxon p-value is 1/1024 = 0.00098,
which is below the smallest Holm threshold for 8 contrasts (0.05/8 = 0.00625), so 8
contrasts are testable.
Thresholds other than the 5% go rule are set on exploratory seeds and then frozen in
a dated analysis plan before the confirmatory run. Post-hoc analyses will be labelled
as such.
10.5 Stylized model M0 (draft; frozen in step 1)
Objects: one effector; n threats (n = 3 to 5); each threat has a value, a breach deadline
and a hidden hardness class (2 to 3 classes) with a known prior; the required dwell per
class is fixed; the effector accumulates progress while dwelling on one threat;
switching costs a slew time.
Observation: the policy sees whether each threat is dead and how much dwell it has
received; a variant adds a noisy progress signal of quality q (the kill-assessment axis).
The belief is a deterministic function of dwell received and the prior.
Objective: maximize the expected value of threats killed before breach.
Solution: DP over per-threat progress levels, time and current target. Expected to be
light for n up to 5 on a coarse dwell grid [OPEN, not measured].
Rules compared: fair allocation, earliest deadline first, least laxity first, shortest
engagement (slew plus expected dwell; Taylor 2021), greedy marginal return, belief
index (Glazebrook et al., Model 1), DP optimum, class-aware oracle.
Regime variables (candidate definitions, fixed in step 1): load ratio = expected total
required dwell over the time available before the last deadline; information = accuracy
or entropy of the class prior; slew ratio = slew time over mean dwell.
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 22 of 25

Output: regret of each rule against the DP optimum over a grid of the three variables.
Go rule: the best simple rule loses at least 5% in at least one region.
10.6 Simulator M1 (outline; specification in step 2)
Laser: melt-through time from Liu et al. (Eqs. 1 to 20) with retargeting time (Eq. 21);
atmosphere from Liu and Zhang; energy and thermal state from Zhang and Li.
High-power microwave: logistic kill probability against a hardness threshold from
Khalil et al., with dwell accumulation. The multi-target cone is our own extension and
will be labelled so.
Sensing: noisy tracking and class confusion in the style of Palmas; random line of sight
in the style of Zhang.
Classes: hardness by material and thickness; roles and maneuvers only as stress
tests.
Interface: Gymnasium; a single central scheduler (no multi-agent training); a setattention policy. Every formula is re-verified against its source in step 2, which
requires the original papers to be re-supplied then.
Parameter ranges are documented with a sensitivity analysis. No claim of calibration.
10.7 Experiments (outline)
Factors: swarm size, arrival pattern, load ratio, slew ratio, pointing and tracking error,
class-observation accuracy, weather, effector mix (laser only versus laser plus
microwave), fraction of hardened targets, prior misspecification.
Design: Latin-hypercube sampling plus a few anchor regimes for the confirmatory
contrasts; one randomized training distribution per RL architecture instead of one
policy per cell.
Out-of-distribution tests: unseen weather, threat mix, swarm size and prior shift.
Ablations: belief input, attention versus masked MLP, masking, domain randomization.
Scale: train at small to moderate sizes; evaluate rules and the optimizer up to 300
where cheap [OPEN].
10.8 Venue strategy
Because the supervisor leaves the venue open, we fix the contribution framing now and
the journal later. C1 and C2 give the theory-light layer for OR readers; C3 gives the
benchmark and sensitivity layer for aerospace and AI readers. Decide the primary venue
Literature Review 2: Directed-Energy Defense Scheduling
Page 23 of 25

family at the end of step 4 (RL pilot), when we know whether the result is 'rules suffice',
'learning helps in region R' or 'a hybrid is needed'. Journal scopes and rankings are not
verified [OPEN].
10.9 Phases, checkpoints and hedged durations
Durations are my unverified estimates, not measurements.
Step 1: freeze the P0 specification and the analysis plan, set up the repository, code
the stylized model and DP, run P0. About 1 to 2 weeks. Checkpoint: the go rule is
applied.
Step 2: simulator specification and unit tests against the published formulas. About 3
to 4 weeks. Checkpoint: formulas reproduced.
Step 3: baselines and oracle, plus a baseline audit. About 2 weeks. Checkpoint: rule
results reproduce the Li and Zhang patterns where applicable.
Step 4: RL pilot and go or no-go. About 2 to 3 weeks. Checkpoint: any region where
learning wins; venue decision.
Step 5: confirmatory runs and out-of-distribution tests. About 4 to 6 weeks.
Step 6: statistics and figures, with scripts that assert plotted values equal table
values. About 2 weeks.
Step 7: writing in the chosen venue's format. About 4 to 6 weeks.
Total, hedged: roughly 18 to 25 weeks.
Pivot rules: if P0 fails the go rule across the grid, switch to the 'when rules suffice' framing
or add structure. If step 4 finds no region where learning wins, the paper reports that,
together with the regime predictor.
10.10 Risks
Rules may be near-optimal everywhere; the paper then reports where and why.
The simulator is ours; mitigated by a randomized simulator family and a sensitivity
analysis.
Overlap with the OR bandit lineage (Glazebrook et al.) and recent OR theory (Chen et
al.); our positioning rests on deadlines, slew, dwell accumulation and the learning
comparison.
Kill-assessment feedback is an assumed observation model; handled as an axis.
Dual use: defender-side only; open-literature parameters; scripted threat behaviors
as stress tests only; no attacker optimization.
